---
title: "signal_score_management (Sherlock Watsons)"
---
stateDiagram-v2
    direction TB
    [*] --> q_init: initialize judge signal score
    q_init --> q_check_eligible: check judging eligibility
    q_check_eligible --> q_eligible: signal >= 10
    q_check_eligible --> q_barred: signal < 10
    q_barred --> q_restore: submit valid issue in audit contest
    q_restore --> q_cap_100: signal restored to max 100
    q_cap_100 --> q_check_eligible: re-check eligibility
    q_eligible --> q_judge_issue: judge votes on issue severity + duplication
    q_judge_issue --> q_contest_resolved: contest judging finalized
    q_contest_resolved --> q_evaluate_votes: for each vote, compare to final result
    q_evaluate_votes --> q_correct: vote matches final judgment
    q_evaluate_votes --> q_incorrect: vote does not match final judgment
    q_correct --> q_increase: signal += reward (weighted by current signal)
    q_incorrect --> q_decrease: signal -= penalty (weighted by current signal)
    q_increase --> q_clamp: clamp signal to [0, 1000]
    q_decrease --> q_clamp: clamp signal to [0, 1000]
    q_clamp --> q_evaluate_votes: next vote
    q_evaluate_votes --> [*]: all votes evaluated, signal updated

    note right of q_check_eligible
        INV-001: Minimum signal to participate = 10
        INV-002: Maximum signal = 1000
        INV-003: Initial signal based on audit + judging leaderboard position
    end note

    note right of q_restore
        INV-004: If signal < 100, submitting valid audit issue restores signal to max 100
        INV-005: Need 10+ signal (i.e. 1 valid issue) to become eligible judge
    end note

    note right of q_increase
        INV-006: Reward magnitude scales with current signal score
        INV-007: Penalty magnitude also scales with current signal score
        INV-008: Higher signal = higher risk and higher reward
    end note
